From 8d4d61de3e775ed832f284caba9c7a8817aa38ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Cormier Roussel <chadcormierroussel@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:59:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] FEXCore: a return into a discarded translation goes to the
 dispatcher

A thread's call-return stack holds host addresses inside translations. An
invalidation empties a thread's stack only when that thread's own lookup
cache had the range (InvalidateThreadCachedCodeRange), and a thread that
entered a block through another thread's link never looked it up: its
next ret ran the old translation of rewritten code. A code buffer refill
empties every stack, which hid it; with translations living longer, Age
of Empires II DE's protector, which rewrites code under its callers, broke
the game's locks.

The JIT now records each call's return point (l_CallReturn) and emits a
16-byte stub per point after the jump thunks, `ldr TMP1, <RIP>; b common;
RIP`, common storing the return block's guest RIP and exiting to the
dispatcher. The block tail lists the points (offsets) and the stubs'
start. Lookup entries keep their translation's start, and
GuestToHostMap::Erase patches every return point of the translation to
branch to its stub, once per translation. Nothing changes on the call or
return path. The offline code cache's mappings have no translation start
and are not patched. Disk cache format 30 -> 31 (the tail changed).

fex-tests callret-stale: a thread that entered A through another thread's
link, parked in A's callee while A is rewritten, returned into the old
code 64/64 (call rel32), 64/64 (call rax), 512/512 (4 threads); 0 with
this change.
